      en: { title: "The application's stock never matches the real stock: what gets built in Algeria in 2027 to make it right, movement ledger, reservations, cycle counting, several warehouses, offline", subtitle: "One product followed for a day in a warehouse, six movements and a figure written nowhere, five layers including the append-only ledger, a transfer between warehouses in six steps with the in-transit state, cycle counting by classes and the month's accuracy, six reasons for wrong stock and what the application does, and the 2027 calendar in six weeks", excerpt: "“The application says 68, the shelf says 66, and nobody knows which to believe.” Wrong stock is not a rigour problem, it is a construction problem: a quantity that can be edited cannot be explained. This guide describes what gets built so that the application's stock is the shelf's: a movement ledger that is never edited and whose quantity is the sum; available distinct from on hand, with reservations that expire; a database lock so the last case is not sold twice; two-step transfers with an in-transit state; movements entered offline with a device identifier, replayed without duplicates; a product record that attaches every barcode; and cycle counting that counts a little every week, without seeing the system figure, to measure the month's accuracy." },
